[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, in today\'s video, not only will I be going over the best new features of WLED version 16, you\'ll also get to see how everything looks on individually addressable strips that have 30 LEDs per meter, a strip that has 60 per meter, and then the granddaddy of them all, a strip that has 144 LEDs per meter. On'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'top of that, the testing will be done where you can see how everything looks on a completely bare strip, as well as diffused using Muzata\'s iconic U-Channel Wave profiles. So, without further ado, let\'s jump in. Now, I won\'t spend too'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'much time on the setup since that\'s not the focus of the video, but for a quick overview in case you\'re curious, I\'m using Gledopto\'s 4D Elite controller that I\'ve used in a ton of my other projects. For power, I\'ll be using a single 12-V 12.5-A supply that will get'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'connected to the left side of the Gledopto controller. And if this was actually a demo for a real project, I would probably connect all my LEDs to the controller by soldering my own wires directly to the pads like I\'m doing here, but since this'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is just for a fun test, I\'ll be using some extension JST connectors I found on Amazon. I\'ll cut off the side I don\'t need, cut the voltage and ground wire shorter, strip back the ends, and get it connected to the controller.'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Then, I\'ll do the same thing for the'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'other two JST connectors since I\'ll be using a total of three strips on three different data outputs. Once that\'s done, everything becomes plug-and-play, and I can quickly get all three rolls connected.'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And then for the demo environment, to try saving my walls for when I take everything down, I\'ll use some white trim pieces, each 8 ft long, and put up three sections using some painter\'s'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tape. Then, using the same tape, I\'ll put up 6-ft long diffuser channels on each of the three sections on the right side. The next day, while everything was still'

[01:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'technically up, it was barely hanging on. So, I ended up putting a couple of brad nails in, making sure to avoid the studs because when I take this all down, the hole should disappear with a coat of paint, no spackling required. Next, I\'ll quickly install the LED'

[02:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'strips. The top one will be our 30 LED per meter strip. The middle will be the 60. And the bottom will be the 144 LED per'

[02:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'meter option. And as always, I\'ll leave links to everything I\'m using in the description for you to check out. Then I snapped on the covers for all three profiles on the right so that the last 6 ft of every run will let us compare the differences between the bare and'

[02:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'diffused LED strips. So, when I go into info, you can see I am running the official version 16 of WLED. Then over in config and LED hardware, I have the brightness limiter'

[02:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'turned on and set to 7,000 milliamps. For my first data output, which is GPIO 16, LED strip type is SK6812, color order GRB. This is the 30 LED per meter strip, which is the one on the top, and'

[02:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'after cutting a few lights off the end, I have 146 total for length, and auto calculate W from RGB is set to dual. My second data output is GPIO 12. Everything is the same as above, but this is my 60 LED per meter strip that\'s'

[03:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in the middle. So, the length after trimming a few lights off the end was 293. And then the last one is the 144 LED per meter strip that\'s on the bottom. And again, everything is the same as the other two, but the length on this one ended up at 704, and the data'

[03:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'output on the controller is GPIO4. Then further down, I checked the box make a segment for each data output and hit save. All right. So, one of my favorite things to do is first turn off the default'

[03:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'color by hitting the black circle. Then go to blink in the effects tab, back to the color tab, and play around with the different color palettes that WLED comes pre-installed with. This is the easiest way to quickly get some awesome gradient lighting to match whatever vibe or mood'

[03:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'re going for. Now, one of my favorite things about V16 is up at the top of your color options. You have this new palette option. This is where you can easily create any custom color palette that you could possibly want. But, scroll further down and you\'ll see'

[03:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that you now have the ability to add over 700 new custom color palettes to your library by simply clicking on the one that you want and hitting the little arrow right here. So, first thing that I would recommend doing is scrolling through these to find some of your'

[04:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'favorites. Then back on your colors tab, scroll down to the bottom and you\'ll see the new additions. And above and beyond the awesome new color palettes, they did tweak the color handling and gamma, so you might notice some improvements in'

[04:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'accuracy. The next feature I want to highlight is found on Pixel Forge, other tools, and then Pixel Paint. At the very top, I can choose the specific segment I want and then it gives me the ability to paint every single LED into the specific color that I want. And the fact that'

[04:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they have all 704 LEDs that are part of segment three charted out here for me to change is crazy. And as I\'m playing around a little more here, want to point out that I\'ll be making a follow-up video in the near future going over the best features of version 16 as it'

[04:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'relates to matrix panels, but for this video, I\'m going to be sticking to the additions that make sense for your traditional LED strips. Moving on, and this one actually might be the one that gets the most people excited because I\'ve gotten so many'

[05:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'comments on how to do a running water effect on power up and shut down in WLED. And thankfully, with this new release, it\'s now super easy. I\'m first going to activate my dedicated white LED that these strips come with by'

[05:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'using the bottom slider. Then turn the lights off. Next, under segments, you have the transaction time that\'s set to 0.7, but under that, you now have a drop-down menu. I\'m going to first change the time to 5 seconds and then choose the swipe right setting.'

[05:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now turn on your lights and you\'ll see this in action. From here, go to presets, click add, and give it a unique name. Go back to segments, and this time from'

[05:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the drop-down, choose swipe left. Turn off the lights and the animations will now go left on power down. Go back to presets, add and give this unique name. Now, if I want to add a button or switch or motion sensor, all'

[05:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of which are supported in WLED, I can simply assign that to one of these presets to create this awesome on off running water effect. And please let me know in the comments if you would like me to do a quick walk through video on those exact steps.'

[06:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The next thing I want to highlight is segment layering and this allows you to get pretty creative with stacking animations together. Here on my 144 LED per meter strip, which is segment two, I have a color twinkle animation running and I have this drop down set to top'

[06:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'default. Now, I can go ahead and add another segment that will essentially be creating a duplicate LED strip for the same LEDs that I have assigned in segment two. Next, with only our new segment selected, I thought it\'d be cool to have'

[06:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the meteor effect with the fire palette going at the same time, so it looks like it\'d be flying through twinkling stars at night. And sure enough, it actually looks pretty awesome. Now, there\'s lots of different layering effects you can play around with, but the general idea is that you can create some pretty'

[06:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'unique and custom animations using this method. So, the last big addition to WLED that I want to go over are the new PS effects, which is short for particle system that you\'ll now see. These are absolutely incredible and something that I\'ve had'

[06:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'way too much fun playing around with. I\'ll first start out with the PS chase and this is a good one to see how the animations look on different densities of LED strips. And for this effect, I can adjust the speed, the density,'

[07:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the size, hue,'

[07:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and the blur. Then, here\'s that same effect on the 144 LED per meter strip on its own. Then, the 60,'

[07:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and finally, the 30. The next new effect you need to check out is the PS pinball. This is probably the most dynamic one and looks so cool'

[07:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on the LED strip because the lights are actually able to bounce off one another and I imagine probably took some pretty advanced coding to come up with this. And I don\'t know about you, but this reminds me so much of air hockey and how the puck glides around and bounces off'

[07:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'other surfaces. PS fire is another incredible addition that I kept coming back to and looks especially impressive on the 144 LED per meter strip. And keep in mind WLED lets you easily reverse direction and or'

[08:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mirror whatever effect. In this instance, I want to reverse it so you can see how the fire looks on the diffuse profiles. And if you\'re new to WLED, you of course'

[08:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can then go to your color section to change it to whatever you want. Another one I really liked was PS hourglass and I think this would be a fun one to have playing in a living room type setting where it wouldn\'t be dominating the room, but instead would'

[08:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'add nice subtle movement for the background. Next, you definitely have to check out PS starburst and this is one that I personally think looks really good on both the 60 and 144 LED per meter strips.'

[08:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And then there\'s about four new PS sound reactive effects that you have to try out. &gt;&gt; [music]'

[09:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; So I will leave a link to the full list of enhancements in the description, but from here on out I\'ll play a lot more footage of everything in action including more close-up shots of the bare LED strips and the diffuser'

[09:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
